## Summary

The paper investigates whether "semantic" evaluation metrics for machine translation provide fundamentally different information than "morphological" (n-gram/edit-based) metrics. By analyzing 11 different metrics across 20 languages using three types of correlation coefficients, the authors conclude that semantic metrics are strongly correlated with morphological ones, suggesting that current embedding-based semantics are essentially a form of high-level morphological statistics. The most important evidence for this is the set of correlation heatmaps and tables (Figure 3, Table 3), which consistently show strong positive correlations (often $>0.7$) between morphological metrics (like BLEU/CHRF) and semantic metrics (like those from Sentence-BERT models) across multiple languages.

